00:00:01.309 --> 00:00:03.379 align:start position:0%
President Trump and congressional  leaders reached a landmark budget deal  avoiding the threat of a historic debt  default the agreement does not address  America's rising debt instead it  increases spending by hundreds of  billions of dollars we Jiang is at the  white house for us so we the president  promised to pay off the debt in eight  years have the lawmakers and the  president given up on dealing with the  debt well it seems like for now that is  on hold the parties don't agree on much  these days but federal spending is  touchy for everybody here because  everyone likes to talk about keeping it  in check but nobody wants to cut  valuable programs and what we are seeing  is all of them coming together in true  Washington fashion to borrow more money  and kick the can down the road this deal  allows the government to do that until  after the 2020 election it suspends the  debt ceiling and boost spending by 320  billion dollars increasing overall  government spending to one point three  seven trillion dollars next year  Republicans are happy to steal in big  military increases while Democrats can  protect domestic programs President  Trump announced the deal on Twitter  saying there were no poison pills  implying that the deal had nothing in it  he was opposed to since Democrats have  agreed not to attach controversial  policy measures to future spending bills  but deficit hawks say the u.s. debt  around 22 trillion dollars is a real  concern arguing future generations will  pay the price now the president  Republicans and Democrats have all  railed against the debt in the past but  gala  no longer seems to be a major issue for  anyone at least for now all right we to  Jane reporting from Washington thank you
 

